  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 211 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over northern\nAlberta/Saskatchewan with trough dropping southward into\ncentral/eastern Montana, while high remains over the upper\nmidwest into the northern edge of the Great Lakes region. Upper\nlevel analysis places low over eastern Kansas into western\nMissouri, while ridge stretches through the Central Rockies into\nthe western side of the Northern Plains. Over our area, low\nclouds continue to encompass much of the area, but these have\ngradually been lifting over the past couple hours.\n\nFor the rest of this afternoon into tonight, lower cloud cover\nshould continue to gradually lift, but there may be a few areas\nwhere it holds well into the evening. Otherwise, some high\nclouds pushing through the upper ridge will pass through. \n\nOn Thursday, a rather quiet day is on tap as warmer air moves\ninto the area with modest upper ridging. High temperatures\nacross western and central North Dakota should warm to about 10\ndegrees above average. As we get into Thursday evening into the\novernight hours, an upper trough sliding over the northern\nRockies makes its approach with its leading short wave pushing\ninto our area. This will bring low to medium chances (about 30\nto 60 percent) of rain showers to northwest and north central\nNorth Dakota. \n\nOn Friday, temperatures remain mild through much of the day, \nbut winds will notably be on the increase as as the \naforementioned upper trough closes off over far eastern \nSaskatchewan into Manitoba, and its affiliated surface low \nrapidly deepens as it slides through Manitoba. Winds will be on\nthe increase over our area, becoming quite gusty. A few rain or\nsnow showers may develop in the evening over and around the\nTurtle Mountain area when the colder air starts to really wrap \nin.\n\nOn Saturday, another breezy to windy day is on tap as the\nsurface low meanders easterly into western Ontario, while cooler\nair continues to wrap into our area, which will result in high\ntemperatures closer to average which may be realized a bit\nearlier in the day. \n\nAs we get into the upcoming week a more tranquil zonal weather \npattern sets up. A small wave may pass through from time to \ntime, but right now the opportunity for precipitation looks to \nbe quite limited. Temperatures look to remain seasonal.",
